Titre$P H O L I O T I N A  -  ARNOLDS in FLORA AG. NERL.  - Clé additionnelle 4
Com$adaptée par Paul Bertéa 9 Octobre 2013
1,0$ KEY 4 : Cheilocystidia subcylindrical, lageniform or utriform, sometimes skittle-shaped but not lecythiform. Veil present or not (ailspecies of Pholiotina).
rem$N.B. This key is an additional key to enable identification of basidiocarps in which the presence or absence of a veil is unclear. See also notes on keys one to three.

1,0 $ ===> This key is an additional key to enable identification of basidiocarps in which the presence or absence of a veil is unclear.
1,0$ Retour au Kit $$f"Pholiotina_kit.cle"
1,0$
1,2 $ Basidiocarps with blue or green tinge on pileus and/or stipe
1,3 $ Basidiocarps without blue or green tinge

2,0 $ Pileus greyish brown to reddish brown without blue colours. Stipe whitish, at base becoming blueish grey to blue-green when bruised and in age. Spores7.0-9.0 x 4.0-5.5 µm $ Ph. cyanopus
2,0 $ Pileus blue-green, at least at centre; stipe whitish; spores 8.0-11.5x4.5-6.5 µm $ Ph. aeruginosa

3,0 $ Pileus plicate-sulcate up to at least half way the radius $ Ph. sulcata
3,4 $ Pileus not plicate-sulcate, often translucently striate or slightly rugulose at centre

4,5 $ Spores on the average longer than 10.0 µm
4,10 $ Spores on the average less than 10.0 µm long

5,6 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly utriform to clavate
5,7 $ Cheilocystidia lageniform or subcylindrical

6,0 $ Basidia 4-spored; spores9.0-11.5 x 5.5-6.5 µm $ Ph. utricystidiata
6,0 $ Basidia 2-spored; spores10.5-14.5 x 5.5-7.5 µm $ Ph. teneroides

7,0 $ Spores on the average longer than 12.0 µm; habitat on dung 20.$ Ph. coprophila
7,8 $ Spores on the average less than 11.5 µm long; habitat on soil

8,0 $ Cheilocystidia versiform, besides lageniform also many utriform, clavate and subcylindrical $ Ph. mutabilis
8,9 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly lageniform with long cylindrical neck, 3.0-5.0(6.0) µm broad

9,0 $ Basidiocarps without distinctive smell; spores on the average 10.4-11.5 µm long; cheilocystidia with cylindrical or tapering neck, not subcapitate; caulocystidia small, 18-38 µm long, lageniform to clavate $ Ph. vexans
9,0 $ Basidiocarps with acidulous smell resembling Pelargonium leaves; spores on the average 9.0-10.6 µm long; cheilocystidia usually in part subcapitate; caulocystidia large, 35-67 µm long, predominantly lageniform $ Ph. nemoralis
9,0,N$/// = P. nemoralis var. dentatomarginata sec Hausknecht (2009), Conocybe-Pholitina:624

10,11 $ Spores without germ pore or only with pale apical spot(callus)
10,12 $ Spores with distinct, although often small germ pore

11,0 $ Basidiocarps usually in spring, rarely in autumn; spores on the average 7.3-9.3 µm long; cheilocystidia often in part subcapitate $ Ph. aporos
11,0 $ Basidiocarps in summer and autumn; spores on the average 6.8-7.9 µm long; cheilocystidia not subcapitate $ Ph. vestita

12,0 $ Cheilocystidia lageniform to sublecythiform, tapering into a narrow neck with well-delimitated globose capitulurn,4.0-6.0 µm wide $ Ph. brunnea
12,13 $ Cheilocystidia subcylindrical, utriform or lageniform, occasionally subcapitate but without well-delimitated capitulum

13,0 $ Spores minutely verruculose under oil-immersion lens, in some collections seemingly smooth (but verruculose as observed with scanning electronmicroscope); cheilocystidia predominantly utriform to clavate, some subcylindrical $ Ph. utriformis
13,14 $ Spore cornpletely smooth; cheilocystidia usually lageniform to lanceolate or subcylindrical, sometimes in part utriform

14,15 $ Spores on the average less than 8.0 µm long
14,20 $ Spores on the average longer than 8.0 µm

15,16 $ Stipe pruinose over entire length, covered with caulocystidia; pileocystidia numerous and striking
15,18 $ Stipe only pruinose at apex, often fibrillose-striate below; pileocystidia absent or very scarce

16, 0 $ Basidiocarps relatively stout: pileus15-45 mm wide, stipe 35-76 x 2-7mm $ Ph. striipes
16,17 $ Basidiocarps tiny: pileus up to10 mm wide, stipe up to 1 mm thick

17,0 $ Cheilocystidia 13-45 x 5.0-8.0 µm $ Ph. mairei
17,0 $ Cheilocystidia 46-66(75) x 9.0-11 µm $ Ph. parvula

18,0 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly subcylindrical to narrowly lageniform, 2.5-8.0 µm wide, with flexuose neck $ Ph. arrhenii
18,19 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly lageniform to utriform, (4.0)6.0-17 µm wide

19,0 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly lageniform with slender, often subcapitate neck; pileus centre usually rather pale when moist: yellow-brown, ochre-brown, orangeybrown $ Ph. velata
19,25 $ Cheilocystidia variable, lageniform, utriform, clavate and subcylindrical; pileus centre reddish brown to rusty brown when moist

20,21 $ Stipe entirely pruinose, covered by caulocystidia; pileocystidia numerous and striking
20,22 $ Stipe pruinose only at extreme apex, often fibrillose-striate below; pileocystidia absent or very scarce and obscure

21,0 $ Basidiocarps relatively stout: pileus 15-55 mm, stipe 2-7 mm thick, lamellae, L=34-45; cheilocystidia lanceolate to lageniform $ Ph. pygmaeoaffinis
21,0 $ Basidiocarps smaller: pileus 7-18(24) mm, stipe 0.5-2 mm thick, lamellae,L=16-22; cheilocystidia subcylindrical to lageniform with cylindrical, often tortuous neck and subacute to subcapitate apex $ Ph. aberrans
21,0,N$///= P. filipes sec Hausknecht (2009), Conocybe-Galerina:578

22,0 $ Cheilocystidia lageniform to lanceolate, gradually tapering into thin neck with obtuse to subacute apex, 1.5-4.0 µm wide, not(sub)capitate $ Ph. rugosa
22,23 $ Cheilocystidia lageniform with cylindrical, thicker neck,3.0-5.0(6.0) µm wide, in part subcapitate, often intermixed with utriform and clavate cells

23,0 $ Cheilocystidia lageniform with long cylindrical neck, often in part subcapitate $ Ph. nemoralis
23,24 $ Cheilocystidia variable, besides lageniform also many utriform and clavate

24,0 $ Spores on the average less than 8.5 µm long $ Ph. exannulata
24,0 $ Spores on the average longer than 9.0 µm $ Ph. mutabilis
24,0,N$/// = P. nemoralis sec Hausknecht (2009), Conocybe-Pholitina:621

25,0 $ With annulate veil on stipe $ Ph. hadrocystis
25,0 $ with denticulate veil on pileus margin $ Ph. exannulata

